ning government ones in the area  many of our children are getting into  trouble especially with drugs we tried  to get support from the government and  pressed them to help us but heard  nothing back this is why we set it up  ourselves we play games and really enjoy  ourselves they teach as well so we like  coming here I want to study and become a  pilot when I grow up they teach us in  English and Urdu I want to learn here  and then become a teacher myself Prime  Minister Imran Khan has vowed to  prioritize and invest in education but  the government continues to spend less  than is recommended by international  standards education should be the  priority of government so even if they  have plies what I personally feel is  where we fail is when we are  implementing it if things are moving in  the same direction  I don't think so we'll be able to  achieve this target within 20 2030 goal  we won't be able to achieve  Sajid says he would love to become a  police man but the only real hope he and  millions like him have is if Pakistan  not only seriously reforms its education  system but also lifts families like  sergeants out of poverty to send their  children to school work cut out  according to the latest UN forecast one  in four children will not complete their  education by 2030 now one of the  organizations that had a hand in setting  these sustainable development goals is  the UN Development Programme its  administrator is former New Zealand  Prime Minister Helen Clark she joins us  from The Hague  welcome to DW getting children into  school and raising an educated workforce  seems like an ambition that everyone can  get behind so what's going wrong what's  going wrong is under investment of  Education the donor community has  stagnated what it is investing in poor  countries for education over the last  nine years or so and it's estimated that  we're about thirty nine billion dollars  short on what would need to be spent to  really reach those those goals the 