ment  and the education of of its people it's  absolutely fundamental and now that  we're living in the world of the  knowledge society where we have to adapt  to new knowledge new technologies all  the time the countries that can't equip  their children with that knowledge and  those skills to acquire knowledge are  just going to get further and further  behind now one of the core principles of  the sustainable development agenda which  every country signed up to was leave no  one behind but on these trends we're  going to be leaving close to a quarter  of a billion children behind that's not  a recipe for peaceful world and that's  why I say it's in our common human  security to be addressing these issues  sorry if I sound like I'm laboring the  point but I'm wondering how you how you  make that sell directly to communities  we saw a poor fishing community there in  the film it's no point talking to them  about taking part in the knowledge  economy when they can't put food on the  table and they can barely find  facilities to to actually put a school  together that's right but education is  one of those investments which can break  through that cycle of poverty a child  with education  got more skills is able to be more  entrepreneurial is able to go to higher  levels of education can send money back  home into the village whether it's from  remittances or from a job in the city or  from a vibrant farm that's that's  actually able to be more productive so  education is the key to getting people  out of poverty people will stay stuck in  poverty if they can't rise out of  illiteracy in a numeracy good talking to  you we wish you well thank you for  joining us Helen Clarke from the UN  Development Programme
 

